Yoga: an entrance through Light on Yoga (@yoga: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @karmic-philosophy, #philosophy @yoga-light-on-yoga, #yoga @yoga-light-on-yoga) { r_017AAD0FQ294F8HBVVTQAZ55GH
Begin with [the reading guide to Light on Yoga](readings/Light-on-Yoga.lingua#r_2N6J2MHG3Q94HBPPNWFKQP0221) and [the identified edition](sources/Light-on-Yoga.lingua#r_7F91WKD2ZF75XE12E244GF0FXF). This small path introduces B. K. S. Iyengar’s account of Yoga through one book, its explanations, and its instructions.

Work from the introductory account of aims and discipline into the descriptions of posture and breathing. The guide asks what the author teaches and how the instruction is organised; its completion concerns understanding rather than demonstrating every physical exercise.

Yoga includes traditions and uses beyond Buddhist contexts. This reading belongs to its own path. Compare a particular question with [Buddhism](../buddhism/Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE) later if useful, preserving differences in terminology, setting, and purpose. Follow `#yoga` connections through an ordinary Protein when desired.

**Return:** [Karmic philosophy: Buddhism and Yoga](../Karmic-philosophy.lingua#r_7FERM3385WX5JGT9X04PNCE85Y).
